\section{Make a Query Key Name a Computation}

An incremental compiler needs more than a cache of previous
answers. It needs to know which inputs were observed when
constructing each answer. Aho and colleagues explain dependency
graphs over attributes of parse-tree occurrences, with edges
specifying which attributes supply others \cite{aho2007compilers}.
Here the graph extends across separately requested compiler
computations, and an execution discovers its own outgoing
dependencies. Diekmann's account of context-sensitive subtree
reuse illustrates why a syntax fragment is not, by itself,
an adequate key for all its semantic computations.

One possible key is \texttt{TypeOf(module,item,target)}, where
the item has stable logical identity within the project and
the target selects target-sensitive rules. Do not put the
workspace revision in every logical key if the intention is
to reuse entries across revisions. Keep a revision stamp
in the entry, or run an invalidation transaction before
serving a new revision. Inputs such as compiler options,
source bytes, package configuration, and generated declarations
must all be explicit fields of keys or observed input queries.
Ordinary file reads through the host environment bypass the
dependency graph and let stale results escape.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Record & Example & Contract \\
\hline
Input key & Value export lookup for \texttt{draw} & Version presence or absence \\
Query key & Type of a particular call & Stable logical computation \\
Memo payload & Type and diagnostics & Immutable result of a successful run \\
Forward edge & Type computation reads lookup & Replace after successful evaluation \\
Reverse edge & Lookup wakes type computation & Mirrors the forward read \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Track Negative and Conditional Reads}

In a toy module system a name lookup first checks a local
declaration index and then tries imports. Initially the local
\texttt{draw} entry is absent and the call resolves to an
import. If an edit adds a local declaration, the previous
imported declaration has not changed, yet the name now
resolves differently. Record the absent local entry or an
explicit version of the index where the lookup occurred.
Likewise, if a language distinguishes value and type
namespaces, querying a value should not unnecessarily depend
on type-only additions. The precision of invalidation is
bounded by these input keys. A whole-module key can be correct
but repeatedly wake queries that do not read the changed names.

\Cref{fig:visual-428} shows both observed reads in the
initial revision. After a local value appears, the query uses
that value and no longer reads the import. The retired edge
is almost as important as the installed one: without removing
it, edits to the import keep causing unrelated recomputations.

