\section{Start With Dirty Propagation and Demand-Driven Repair}

\begin{verbatim}
commit_inputs(edits):
    # No queries run during this transaction.
    changed := install_input_values(edits)
    todo := queue_of(changed)
    seen := set_of(changed)
    while not todo.empty():
        key := todo.pop()
        for user in reverse_reads[key]:
            memo[user].dirty := true
            if seen.insert_if_absent(user): todo.push(user)

get(key):
    if is_input(key): return input_value(key)
    if key in active: report_query_cycle(active, key)
    if memo[key] exists and not memo[key].dirty:
        return memo[key].value
    reads := empty_set()
    with active.push_until_exit(key):
        result := evaluate(key, read = function(dep):
            value := get(dep)
            reads.insert(dep)
            return value)
    publish_result_and_replace_edges(key, result, reads)
    return result
\end{verbatim}

The sketch is single-threaded, uses a stable snapshot for
the whole evaluation, and treats a cycle as failure rather
than an ordinary cacheable result. A diagnostic result that
the query deliberately computes as a normal value can be
memoized, with its observed reads. The read callback tracks a
dependency whether the producer was fresh or already cached.
Publication compares the old and new read sets to update
reverse edges, installs the new value, and clears dirty.
Failure leaves the old edge set and dirty status in place.
If publication involves fallible allocation, reserve space
or delay replacement until all pieces can be installed.

An active stack cycle tells more than an arbitrary maximal
component of the old dependency graph. Report the cycle
along the current request chain. If recursion is legal and
must produce a least fixed point, specify a separate solver
for that family of mutually recursive queries. Returning an
unrelated cached value merely to break the cycle hides a
semantic choice about recursion. Concurrent evaluation needs
additional ownership and waiting rules; two threads can
wait on each other's active entries even when their own
thread-local stacks contain no repeated key.

\section{Do Not Confuse a Verified Result With a Changed Result}

The sketch deliberately dirties the whole reverse cone and
does not stop downstream reruns when a result turns out
equal. A later refinement can track both the last revision
verified and the last revision changed. For example, a
comment edit can change a parse result but leave exported
types equal; downstream type checks need only the latter.
This optimization requires a definition of equality that
covers every observable field of the shared result, including
diagnostic ranges if those are in the value. A useful design
separates semantic payloads from revision-specific diagnostic
projection so unchanged payloads can feed new ranges.
Do not conflate the two stamps or certify downstream entries
before their required dependencies are verified.

Use a fresh build as the test oracle after each edit. Test
the failed-local lookup becoming successful, a subsequent
import edit that should not dirty the new result, and removal
of the local entry to restore import lookup. Cancel evaluation
midway through recording reads and ensure the result was
not marked clean. Track invalidation cone sizes, nodes
actually requested, equal recomputed payloads, and memory
retained by old keys or abandoned snapshots. For a large
project it can be cheaper to evict a coherent set of memos
and rebuild them than to retain a graph no current editor
view will request, but eviction must not make a dependent
look clean after the only invalidation edge is discarded.

\section{Choose What Eviction Promises to Existing Readers}

Consider evicting the result of \texttt{Find(draw)} while
its consumer \texttt{TypeCall} is still clean. Removing the
producer's outgoing edges completely now breaks the path
from the missing local declaration to \texttt{TypeCall}.
There are two useful answers. Keep a compact dependency
tombstone for the producer even after discarding its value,
and propagate dirtiness through that entry just as through
a full memo. Or mark and evict the producer and mark its
reverse cone dirty while the old edges are still available.
Discarding both value and edges without doing either creates
a stale cache no matter how good the query keys are.

For a tombstone implementation, distinguish ``no value but
edges known'' from ``no entry.'' A demanded former producer
recomputes and installs both its new answer and its exact
read set. The tombstone has a maintenance cost; if it
retains whole ASTs indirectly through keys, it fails as a
memory optimization. Track retained bytes of read lists
and key metadata, not just values removed.

Test an edit sequence that evicts the lookup before a local
\texttt{draw} is added, then requests only \texttt{TypeCall}.
The type must follow the new local declaration. Follow with
a request for an unrelated function whose dependencies
are still intact to ensure the strategy didn't silently
disable memoization. Large-cone invalidation and compact
tombstones offer different time and space tradeoffs; test
both edit latency and the latency of the first later query.
